      <div class="container mx-auto max-w-4xl relative z-10">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4">
          Fires and Squatters in Vacant Buildings: Protecting Empty Property in Seattle and Tacoma This Winter
        </h1>
        <div class="flex items-center text-lg mb-8">
          <time datetime="2026-10-04">October 4, 2026</time>
          <span class="mx-4">&bull;</span>
          <span>16 min read</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
          Just after midnight on Friday, October 2, Tacoma Fire crews pulled up to a boarded-up house in the 600 block of East 32nd Street and found it fully engulfed. They fought it from outside first and evacuated nearby homes as a precaution. Crews stayed through the night chasing hot spots. Nobody was hurt. One neighbor's siding got damaged, and the cause is still under investigation. That block is about a mile from our office on East 26th. Empty buildings burning barely makes news in Seattle or Tacoma anymore, which is kind of the problem. Winter is coming and clocks fall back November 1, so here is what owners of vacant property in the Puget Sound actually need to know: what the cities expect, what Washington law says about squatters, the insurance clause that quietly shrinks your coverage after 60 days, and where cameras help.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quick Answer Section -->
    <QuickAnswer
      question="How do you keep a vacant building in Seattle or Tacoma from being broken into or burned?"
      answer="Close every opening so it can't be entered, which Seattle's vacant building code and the fire code Washington adopts statewide both require, and keep the lot clear of junk and dry trash. Tell your insurer the day the building goes empty, because standard commercial property policies stop paying for vandalism and theft after 60 consecutive vacant days and cut other claims by 15%. Inspect on a schedule and keep dated photos. In Washington, police can remove squatters from residential property under RCW 9A.52.105 only after the owner signs a declaration that, among other things, the premises were not abandoned when the person entered. Then add monitoring that doesn't depend on the building's power, such as a solar surveillance trailer on cellular, so somebody sees a break-in while it is still only a trespass. Seattle and Tacoma police both give weight to calls backed by live video. General information only, so check specifics with your attorney and insurance agent."
    />

    <!-- Main Content -->
